NAME
    dump - write a raw memory range to disk

SYNOPSIS
    dump <space> <addr> <len> <file>

DESCRIPTION
    Writes <len> raw bytes from <space> starting at <addr> to
    home/Logs/<CoreName>/<file> - no header or
    metadata, so a hex editor can open the result directly. The write-side
    counterpart is 'load'. Same live-hardware-space refusal as
    'search'/'snapshot'.

    The file name is taken from home/Logs/<CoreName>/: a bare name or one
    with '../' in it is resolved from there, one with a leading '/' from
    this shell's root, as every other command reads a path (see 'man
    hier'). A name that lands above the root is refused, so '../../../x'
    and an absolute path of the machine cannot write or read outside it.
    Before 2026-10-10 the name was joined to the folder unchecked, and
    both did.

EXAMPLES
    dump WRAM 0 2000 wram.bin
    dump WRAM 0 2000 /tmp/wram.bin
